// @error An import specifier is a string — single quotes are a char literal; write "std:fs"
// @error Char literal must contain exactly one character
// CONTRACT (must-error): the SINGLE-SOURCE spelling of the char-quoted
// specifier. `import x from '…'` has no line-leading `import {`, so the host's
// textual gate keeps it off the module path entirely and the PARSER is the only
// scanner that sees it — it used to answer `malformed import: expected a module
// path string literal` anchored at the `import` keyword, which describes a
// statement with no specifier rather than one with the wrong quotes.
//
// The sentence is the module front end's, shared (`importSpecCharMsg`), because
// the same mistake must not read two ways depending on which scanner ran.
//
// The lexer's own length error fires beside it and that is intended: both are
// about the same literal, both now span it, and the pair reads as one finding
// with its cause. (A one-character specifier — `from 'x'` — draws this error
// alone, since the literal itself is well formed.)
import fs from 'std:fs'

print("after")
